<!-- An edge node for several local nodes (conf/multi-domain/README.md; plans/Edge-Node2.md §4.1): one small cloud
    instance in front of several local nodes, such as branch offices or the separate organizations of one group, each
    on its own database with its own domains. Each local node pairs with a code of its own and is a pairing here; this
    node routes each name, address, number, bridge, domain and site to the pairing that claimed it first, and refuses a
    later claim with "another node has this name". It is conf/edge/MoquiEdgeConf.xml with what changes for several
    pairings said in each area; System › Edge Clients shows each pairing (Clients), what it claims (Domains) and its
    tunnel ports (Ports).
    Isolation is reasonable, not multi-tenant: one operator runs this node for related pairings, and a mistake or an
    overload in one domain should not break or leak into another. Kept apart: every edge row is keyed by pairing, the
    queue's size cap, the streams and tunnels of each link, a share of the mail delivery, call legs, and a TURN secret
    each. Shared: this node's addresses and mail reputation, the DKIM key, the media and TURN ports, web protection's
    counts, CPU and bandwidth.
    Build, archive and run with the release flag of ./moq: the Maximal package, or Enterprise with an external database.
    The runtime has the base components and mr-pass only (./moq component-get edge). Per-host settings go in .env. -->
<moqui-conf xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="http://moqui.org/xsd/moqui-conf-3.xsd">
    <default-property name="instance_purpose" value="production"/>
    <!-- takes links from paired local nodes on the HTTPS listener, told from browsers by ALPN; an edge node has no items
        of its own, so every item has an owner among the pairings -->
    <default-property name="node_role" value="edge"/>
    <!-- rate limits, connection limits and bans by client address, for every pairing's names together. With a proxy in
        front, name it in webapp_trusted_proxies (.env) -->
    <default-property name="webapp_web_protection" value="true"/>

    <!-- The link and web access (.env): webapp_http_host, this node's own public name, and edge_public_addresses, its
        public IPv4 and IPv6 addresses, which each local node's wizard checks its A and AAAA records against. A WebPKI
        certificate for this node's own name in runtime/cert, and for each terminate host name of any pairing. Each
        pairing's host names go to it alone: its wizard asks for them, or the operator adds them on Edge Clients ›
        Domains; a name another pairing has is refused. A delegated call goes to the pairing whose item it is (the owner
        scope), and one with no owner is refused while more than one pairing is paired. -->

    <!-- SSH: edge_ssh_tunnel_port goes to the one active pairing only, and is refused with several (and while that one
        is away), so with several give each its own port on Edge Clients › Ports (a moqui.edge.EdgePort row, listening on edge_ssh_tunnel_host, every address when empty). Open
        each port in the firewall. -->

    <!-- PASS (plans/Edge-Node2.md §4.1): mr-pass here asks for the authority by site. Each local node's Configure names
        its PASS sites, and a call for a site goes to the pairing that names it; a site no pairing names goes to the one
        pairing when there is one, and is refused with several. Set pass_backend_web_url in .env to the web address,
        through this node, of the pairing whose sign-in mr-pass's front channel uses. -->
    <default-property name="pass_backend_url" value="edge:"/>

    <!-- XMPP (docs/interfaces/XMPP.md, Behind an edge node): one pair of ports takes every pairing's XMPP domain, by the
        TLS server name on direct TLS and by the stream's to on STARTTLS; each local node sets its own xmpp_domain, and
        a domain another pairing named first is refused. Set in .env:
                                     <default-property name="edge_xmpp_port" value="5222"/>
                                     <default-property name="edge_xmpp_tls_port" value="5223"/>
        Each domain's _xmpp-client._tcp and _xmpps-client._tcp records name this node. A pairing's own pair of ports is
        an option: two rows on Edge Clients › Ports. -->

    <!-- Mail (docs/interfaces/Mail-Inbound.md, The edge node as the gateway): the MTA in moqui takes port 25 for every
        pairing's mail domains; set smtp_listen_port 25, smtp_listen_host 0.0.0.0 and smtp_hostname (the MX name of
        every domain) in .env. RCPT TO is answered from the pairings' replicated recipients: an address goes to the
        pairing whose copies have it, the most specific match first (joe+x@ before joe@), and an address two pairings
        both have gets 451 and is logged until one lets it go. A domain may be split between pairings by address. Mail
        out is signed with the one DKIM key, published in each domain's DNS, and only for the pairing's own domains; the
        delivery gives each pairing with mail due an even share of its sessions, so one pairing's backlog does not hold
        the others' mail. Bounces come back to edge_mail_bounce_domain, shared. -->
    <default-property name="edge_mail_mta" value="moqui"/>
    <default-property name="smtp_internet" value="true"/>
    <default-property name="edge_mail_relay_host" value="127.0.0.1"/>
    <default-property name="edge_mail_relay_port" value="10025"/>
    <smtp-server max-connections="100" max-connections-per-ip="5" connections-per-minute="30"/>

    <!-- Chat bridges and SMS (docs/interfaces/Chat-Bridges.md): ids come from separate databases and two pairings may
        have the same one, so a webhook goes by the request's host name first. At a pairing's terminate host name only
        its bridges answer, queued while it is away; at this node's own names the one pairing with the id answers, and
        an id two pairings have gets 401. A local node whose ids may collide sets channels_public_url to one of its
        terminate hosts, so its networks call that name. -->
    <default-property name="channels_webhooks_enabled" value="true"/>

    <!-- Calls (docs/interfaces/WebRTC.md, TURN.md): this node is the media node and TURN of every pairing's calls. Each
        pairing gets a TURN secret of its own, made from turn_auth_secret and its id and handed over the link, so a
        revoked pairing loses its TURN access with nothing rotated. edge_call_legs_per_pairing caps one pairing's legs
        at once (200 by default). Set rtc_public_address in .env; open UDP 10000, UDP and TCP 3478 and the relay range
        turn_relay_ports (49152-65535). -->
    <default-property name="rtc_media_port" value="10000"/>
    <default-property name="rtc_media_host" value="0.0.0.0"/>
    <default-property name="turn_port" value="3478"/>
    <default-property name="call_recording_segment_mb" value="16"/>

    <!-- SIP (docs/interfaces/SIP.md, Behind an edge node): every pairing's trunks register here, each keyed by its
        pairing; an inbound call goes to the pairing whose copies have the number, and a number two pairings have is
        refused (404, logged). Phones register in their pairing's realm (its sip_domain, named in its Configure); a
        realm another pairing named first is refused. Set sip_port, sip_tls_port, sip_host and sip_public_address in .env
        as for one local node, and sip_registrar_enabled=true for outside phones. -->

    <!-- Backups: the link's queue (what waits for each pairing while it is away) is the only data here that matters.
        This sets the paused seed job backup_EmbeddedDatabase_daily running at each boot (docs/Run-Deploy.md, Backups). -->
    <default-property name="embedded_db_backup_scheduled" value="true"/>

    <entity-facade crypt-pass="${entity_ds_crypt_pass}" query-stats="true"/>

    <!-- The queue: what waits for a pairing that is away is kept 14 days, and at most queue-max-megabytes of it for
        each pairing alone, so one pairing's backlog does not crowd out another's; a pairing's own cap
        (EdgeClient.queueMaxMegabytes, Edge Clients › Clients, Queue cap) takes its place, larger for a busy branch or
        smaller for a small one. Past it the pairing's oldest fail, logged. -->
    <edge queue-retention-days="14" queue-max-megabytes="1024" queue-max-attempts="10" queue-retry-seconds="5"/>
</moqui-conf>
